# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
berkeley-master.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: berkeley-master.logic.r2 has "framing_ideas"; berkeley-master.logic.r2.2 has "imagine_unperceived → framing_ideas"; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows; berkeley-master.logic.r2.3 has "imagine_unperceived" and "framing_ideas"
berkeley-master.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps berkeley-master.logic.r2 and berkeley-master.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "one can easily imagine trees in a park or books in a closet with nobody by to perceive them" and "imagining them is only framing in your mind ideas you call books and trees while omitting to frame the idea of anyone who may perceive them" are false. Is S2, "I answer, you may so, there is no difficulty in it; but what is all this, I beseech you, more than framing in your mind certain ideas which you call BOOKS and TREES, and the same time omitting to frame the idea of any one that may perceive them?", true then?
berkeley-master.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: berkeley-master.logic.r2 has "repugnant → ¬conceive_unconceived" and "shows_mind_indep → conceive_mind_indep"; berkeley-master.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-master.logic.r2.3 have "framing_ideas ∧ perceive_while → ¬shows_mind_indep" and "repugnant → ¬conceive_unconceived"; they differ when only "imagining this shows that you can conceive it possible the objects of your thought may exist without the mind", "you can conceive it possible the objects of your thought may exist without the mind", "imagining them is only framing in your mind ideas you call books and trees while omitting to frame the idea of anyone who may perceive them", "you yourself perceive or think of them all the while" and "you conceive them existing unconceived or unthought of" are true, which the first allows
berkeley-master.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: berkeley-master.logic.r2 has "¬shows_mind_indep"; berkeley-master.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-master.logic.r2.3 have "¬conceive_mind_indep"; they differ when only "you can conceive it possible the objects of your thought may exist without the mind" is true, which the first allows
berkeley-master.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: berkeley-master.logic.r2 puts it at S4, "This therefore is nothing to the purpose; it only shows you have the power of imagining or forming ideas in your mind: but it does not show that you can conceive it possible the objects of your thought may exist without the mind."; berkeley-master.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-master.logic.r2.3 at S5, "To make out this, IT IS NECESSARY THAT YOU CONCEIVE THEM EXISTING UNCONCEIVED OR UNTHOUGHT OF, WHICH IS A MANIFEST REPUGNANCY."
berkeley-master.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-master.logic.r2, to: Is the text's main conclusion S4, "This therefore is nothing to the purpose; it only shows you have the power of imagining or forming ideas in your mind: but it does not show that you can conceive it possible the objects of your thought may exist without the mind.", rather than S5, "To make out this, IT IS NECESSARY THAT YOU CONCEIVE THEM EXISTING UNCONCEIVED OR UNTHOUGHT OF, WHICH IS A MANIFEST REPUGNANCY."?
berkeley-master.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: berkeley-master.logic.r2 and berkeley-master.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if imagining this shows that you can conceive it possible the objects of your thought may exist without the mind, then you can conceive it possible the objects of your thought may exist without the mind"; berkeley-master.logic.r2.2 does not
berkeley-master.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-master.logic.r2 and berkeley-master.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if imagining this shows that you can conceive it possible the objects of your thought may exist without the mind, then you can conceive it possible the objects of your thought may exist without the mind"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
